# LeetCode 153. Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array


                                         ******************************************************************************
                                                                # Approach 1: Brute Force
                                         ******************************************************************************


## Intuition

// The simplest approach is to scan the entire array and keep track of the minimum element.
//
// Since every element is checked exactly once, we are guaranteed to find the smallest value.
//
// Although this approach is very easy to implement, it ignores the fact that the array is sorted and rotated.



## Algorithm

// 1. Assume the first element is the minimum.
// 2. Traverse the entire array.
// 3. Update the minimum whenever a smaller element is found.
// 4. Return the minimum element.



## C++ Code


class Solution {
public:
    int findMin(vector<int>& nums) {

        // Assume first element is the minimum.
        int minimum = nums[0];

        // Traverse the entire array.
        for (int num : nums) {

            // Update minimum if a smaller element is found.
            minimum = min(minimum, num);
        }

        // Return the smallest element.
        return minimum;
    }
};


## Time Complexity

// * Traverse all elements once = O(n)

// Overall: O(n)



## Space Complexity   O(1)



## Why is it Slow?

// This approach completely ignores the sorted nature of the array.

// If
// * n = 10^5

// then all 100000 elements must be visited even though the answer can be found much faster using Binary Search.


                                         ******************************************************************************
                                                   # Approach 2: Linear Search for Rotation Point
                                         ******************************************************************************


## Intuition

// In a rotated sorted array, the minimum element is exactly where the increasing order breaks.

// Example:

// 4 5 6 7 0 1 2
//         ^

// Here, 0 is the first element that is smaller than its previous element.

// If no such position exists, then the array was never rotated, so the first element is the minimum.



## Algorithm

// 1. Traverse the array from index 1.
// 2. Compare every element with its previous element.
// 3. If nums[i] < nums[i-1], return nums[i].
// 4. If no such position exists, return nums[0].



## C++ Code


class Solution {
public:
    int findMin(vector<int>& nums) {

        int n = nums.size();

        // Find the first position where sorted order breaks.
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {

            // Rotation point found.
            if (nums[i] < nums[i - 1])
                return nums[i];
        }

        // Array is already sorted.
        return nums[0];
    }
};


## Time Complexity

// * Traverse the array once = O(n)

// Overall: O(n)



## Space Complexity  O(1)


## Why is it Better?

// Instead of computing the minimum throughout the array,
// this approach immediately returns when the rotation point is found.

// However, in the worst case (already sorted array), it still scans all elements.


                                         ******************************************************************************
                                         # Approach 3: Optimal Binary Search
                                         ******************************************************************************


## Intuition

// Since the array is sorted and then rotated, at least one half of the array is always sorted.

// Observation:

// * If nums[mid] > nums[right],
//   then the minimum lies in the right half.

// * Otherwise,
//   the minimum lies in the left half (including mid).

// Using this property, we eliminate half of the search space after every iteration.



## Algorithm

// 1. Initialize:

//    * left = 0
//    * right = n - 1

// 2. While left < right:

//    * Find mid.
//    * If nums[mid] > nums[right]:

//         Minimum is in the right half.

//    * Otherwise:

//         Minimum is in the left half (including mid).

// 3. Continue until left == right.

// 4. Return nums[left].



## C++ Code


class Solution {
public:
    int findMin(vector<int>& nums) {

        // Search space boundaries.
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.size() - 1;

        // Binary Search.
        while (left < right) {

            // Find middle index.
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            // Minimum lies in the right half.
            if (nums[mid] > nums[right]) {

                left = mid + 1;
            }
            // Minimum lies in the left half (including mid).
            else {

                right = mid;
            }
        }

        // left == right points to the minimum element.
        return nums[left];
    }
};


## Time Complexity

// Binary Search halves the search space in every iteration.

// Number of iterations = O(log n)

// Overall: O(log n)



## Space Complexity    O(1)



## Why is this Optimal?

// * The array is partially sorted, allowing Binary Search.
// * Every iteration discards half of the remaining search space.
// * Since at least one comparison is required per level of Binary Search,
//   O(log n) is the best possible time complexity.
// * Only constant extra space is used.



# Complexity Comparison

| Approach                         | Time Complexity | Space Complexity |
| -------------------------------- | --------------- | ---------------- |
| Brute Force                      | O(n)            | O(1)             |
| Linear Search for Rotation Point | O(n)            | O(1)             |
| Optimal Binary Search            | **O(log n)**    | **O(1)**         |
